// ============================================================================
// SELLAPAGE — FIRESTORE SECURITY RULES
// ============================================================================
// Last updated: 2026-09-18 (stores create closed, phone-first signup)
//
// CHANGE LOG (newest first, so it is obvious what still needs publishing)
//   2026-09-26  Dropshipping Marketplace, Phase 2 (supplier listings).
//               stores/{id}/products: `allow write` split into create /
//               update / delete so the marketplace fields (listing, wholesale
//               and minimum prices, category, NAFDAC number, and the Phase 3
//               dropshipper-copy fields) cannot be set or changed from a
//               browser. Everything else about products is unchanged for the
//               owner. Added `supplierTermsVersion` and
//               `supplierTermsUpdatedAt` to verificationFields, and
//               `match /supplierTerms/{supplierId}` (+ versions), server only.
//               SAFE TO PUBLISH ANY TIME: no browser code writes any of these.
//   2026-09-25  Dropshipping Marketplace, Phase 1 (supplier onboarding).
//               Locked the supplier* fields (verificationFields): status,
//               applied/approved/rejected/suspended timestamps, the reasons,
//               the application video and who decided. Written only by
//               /api/supplier-application (the vendor applying) and
//               /api/admin-marketplace (the decision), both on the Admin SDK.
//               SAFE TO PUBLISH ANY TIME: no client writes any of them today,
//               and the marketplace itself stays shut until the stage is
//               moved off 'coming_soon' in Admin > Dropshipping > Access.
//   2026-09-23  Locked `marketplaceTester` (verificationFields). It grants a
//               store early access to the Dropshipping Marketplace while
//               DROPSHIPPING_STAGE is 'testing', so it must never be
//               client-writable. Safe to publish any time: it only tightens a
//               path no client uses. Set it by hand in the Firebase console
//               (or from the admin panel once Phase 1 ships).
//   2026-09-19  Dropshipping Marketplace, Phase 0 (coming soon + waitlist).
//               Added top-level `match /marketplaceWaitlist/{entryId}`, server
//               only. SAFE TO PUBLISH ANY TIME: no client reads or writes it.
//               `stores/{id}.marketplaceInterest` ({ supply, dropship }) is
//               deliberately NOT locked: it records interest only and grants
//               nothing, and Settings writes it from the browser. The fields
//               that WILL grant access (supplierStatus and the marketplace
//               product fields) are locked in Phase 1 and 2, before any code
//               trusts them.
//   2026-09-18  /stores `allow create` CLOSED (`allow create: if false`).
//               Stores are created only by /api/signup-phone (Admin SDK) after
//               the SMS code is verified. The website stopped writing the store
//               from the browser in commit cb4490c. The Play Store app's old
//               signup (client-side store write) was already not working, so
//               nothing that works today is broken by this; the app's next
//               release must use /api/signup-phone (Docs/App-Phone-Signup-Handoff.md).
//               ORDER: publish AFTER cb4490c is Ready on Vercel. Before that,
//               the old web bundle still creates stores from the browser.
//               The old create rule's pinned values (plan starter, no money /
//               verification / integration / design fields, product caps) are
//               gone with it: nothing a client sends can create a store now.
//   2026-09-18  Phone-first signup. Added `match /signupChallenges/{phone}`
//               and `match /verifiedPhones/{phone}`, both server only. Default
//               deny already covered both; they are listed so nobody mistakes
//               "no rule" for an oversight. SAFE TO PUBLISH ANY TIME.
//   2026-09-18  Leads: client `create` CLOSED (`allow create: if false`).
//               Storefront leads now go through /api/lead-submit on the Admin
//               SDK, which notifies the vendor, rate-limits per store, and
//               reads storeName from the store instead of trusting the
//               browser. Read rule unchanged.
//               ORDER-CRITICAL: publish ONLY AFTER the deploy containing
//               lead-submit.js (commit f64ad00) shows Ready on Vercel. Before
//               that, storefronts still run the old bundle, which writes leads
//               from the browser, and this rule would reject every one.
//               Also added top-level `match /webhookAudit/{docId}`, server
//               only. Written by sendbox-webhook.js and topship-webhook.js to
//               keep a permanent record of courier statuses (Vercel Hobby
//               keeps logs one hour). Default deny already covered it; the
//               explicit block is so nobody mistakes "no rule" for an
//               oversight. Holds raw courier payloads, which include customer
//               addresses, so it must never become readable.
//               No change needed for the other new server data:
//               stores/{id}/private/notificationPrefs and
//               stores/{id}/meta/digests + meta/teamActivity sit under the
//               existing server-only `private` and `meta` blocks.
//   2026-09-18b Email Broadcast: top-level `emailCampaigns` (with its
//               `recipients` subcollection), `emailSuppressions` and
//               `emailQuota`, all server only. Default deny already covers
//               them; explicit so nobody reads "no rule" as an oversight.
//               Safe to publish any time: no client reads or writes them.
//   PUBLISHED   Every entry below, up to and including 2026-09-11, was
//               published by Nex (confirmed 2026-09-12). The "NOT YET
//               PUBLISHED" notes below are kept as history.
//   2026-09-11  Mobile push. Added top-level `match /devices/{deviceId}` and
//               `match /broadcasts/{broadcastId}`, both server only, plus
//               `match /notifications/{notificationId}` (owner read) and
//               `match /meta/{docId}` (server only) inside /stores/{storeId}.
//               NOT YET PUBLISHED.
//               ORDER: publish these BEFORE the app ships, because `devices`
//               holds FCM registration tokens and `broadcasts` holds the plan
//               filters a message was targeted with. Nothing legitimate reads
//               either from a browser - every path goes through
//               /api/device-register, /api/device-unlink, /api/notifications
//               and /api/admin-push on the Admin SDK - so publishing early
//               breaks nothing and publishing late leaves tokens exposed.
//               Note the token exposure this FIXES: `fcmToken` still sits on
//               stores/{storeId}, which is `allow read: if true`, so every
//               vendor's push token is world readable today. That field stays
//               during the transition and should be deleted once every client
//               writes to the registry instead.
//   2026-09-10  Locked the TikTok CONNECTION fields (integrationFields):
//               tiktokConnected, tiktokUsername, tiktokAvatarUrl,
//               tiktokProfileLink, tiktokIsVerified, tiktokFollowerCount,
//               tiktokLikesCount, tiktokVideoCount, tiktokVideos,
//               tiktokVideosSyncedAt, tiktokStatsSyncedAt. Written ONLY by
//               tiktok-callback.js and tiktok-account.js on the Admin SDK.
//               NOT YET PUBLISHED. Load-bearing: these render on a PUBLIC
//               storefront as social proof, so a client-writable
//               tiktokFollowerCount would let any vendor type 900,000
//               followers onto their own store page, and a writable
//               tiktokVideos would let them put arbitrary image urls and
//               links in front of their customers. Same reasoning as
//               cacVerified: Sellapage is making the claim, so Sellapage
//               writes it.
//               `tiktokPixelId` is deliberately NOT locked - it is public by
//               design, exactly like metaPixelId, and the TikTok tab writes it
//               from the browser. Safe to publish any time: no client writes
//               any of the locked fields.
//   2026-09-08  Added top-level `match /cacRequests/{requestId}`, server only.
//               NOT YET PUBLISHED. Holds vendor email and phone.
//   2026-09-05  Added `match /abandonedCheckouts/{reference}` inside
//               /stores/{storeId}. NOT YET PUBLISHED as of writing. Must go live
//               BEFORE the first record is written, since these documents hold
//               customer name, email, phone and basket contents.
//   2026-09-05  orders and bookings: `delete` denied, create/update unchanged.
//               NOT YET PUBLISHED as of writing. The dashboard delete buttons
//               were removed the same day, but the client SDK path stayed open
//               until this lands. Nothing legitimate deletes them: the only two
//               callers were the buttons just removed, and no server handler
//               deletes an order. Safe to publish before or after the code.
//   2026-09-05  Locked `previousSlugs` (verificationFields). Written only by
//               store-seo.js when a vendor renames their store URL; the
//               storefront renderer 301s those old addresses to the current
//               one. Client-writable would mean a vendor could list another
//               store's slug and hijack it on rename. Safe to publish any time:
//               no client writes this field.
//   2026-09-04  Locked `emailVerifiedAt` and `gateExempt` (verificationFields).
//               `emailVerifiedAt` is written ONLY by login-verify.js when an
//               emailed OTP clears, and the new storefront EMAIL gate trusts it
//               to decide public visibility. If a browser could set it, an
//               abandoned or bot signup would simply publish itself and the
//               gate would be decorative. Safe to publish at any time: it only
//               tightens a path no legitimate client uses.
//   2026-09-01  Added `match /loyalty/{code}` inside /stores/{storeId}.
//               NOT YET PUBLISHED as of writing. Must go live BEFORE any store
//               switches loyalty on, because the document id is the customer's
//               loyalty code and that code spends money.
//   2026-08-12  Locked the phone verification fields (see the note below).
//   2026-08-09  Security review remediation: C-06, H-07, M-05.
//
// DEPLOYMENT: copy this file's contents (everything below the header block)
// into Firebase Console > Firestore > Rules, or run:
//     firebase deploy --only firestore:rules
//
// 2026-08-12 UPDATE — phone verification fields are now locked. This change
// only TIGHTENS an update path no legitimate client uses (they are written
// server-side by phone-verify.js via the Admin SDK, which bypasses rules), so
// it is safe to publish before or after the application code.
//
// ⚠️ SEPARATE, ORDER-CRITICAL: the storefront phone gate.
// `ENABLE_STOREFRONT_PHONE_GATE=true` hides every store WITHOUT a verified
// phone. Every store that exists today has no phoneVerified field, so enabling
// it before grandfathering would hide the ENTIRE platform. Correct order:
//     1. Fix TERMII_SENDER_ID (currently a UUID — must be the sender NAME)
//     2. Wait for Termii approval, confirm a real SMS arrives
//     3. node scripts/backfill-phone-gate-exempt.js --dry-run   (then for real)
//     4. ONLY THEN set ENABLE_STOREFRONT_PHONE_GATE=true
// The gate is OFF by default, so doing nothing changes nothing.
//
// ⚠️ THE SAME ORDER APPLIES TO THE NEW EMAIL GATE.
// `ENABLE_STOREFRONT_EMAIL_GATE=true` hides every store without a server-set
// `emailVerifiedAt`. Existing stores have none, so grandfather them FIRST
// (phoneGateExempt or gateExempt both satisfy it) or the whole platform
// disappears. It is independent of the phone gate and needs no Termii, so it
// can be switched on much sooner - but not before the backfill.
//
// ⚠️ DEPLOY ORDER MATTERS. Ship the application code FIRST, then these rules.
// Two client writes were moved server-side to make the field locks below
// possible, and deploying rules ahead of that code will break both flows:
//   1. CACVerificationTab.jsx now calls  POST /api/verify-cac?action=confirm
//      instead of writing `cacVerified` directly.
//   2. Dashboard.jsx no longer writes the legacy plan-field backfill; it
//      derives those values in memory instead.
//
// DESIGN NOTES
// - The Admin SDK (all files in src/api-handlers/) BYPASSES these rules
//   entirely. Every lock below constrains browsers only; server handlers are
//   unaffected. This is why fields can be locked here without breaking any
//   server-side write path.
// - Firestore has NO field-level read security. Anything on a document with
//   `allow read: if true` is world-readable. Secrets therefore live in
//   stores/{id}/private/* (see src/api-handlers/_lib/store-secrets.js).
// - `allow read` grants BOTH `get` and `list`. `list` cannot be disabled on
//   /stores because getStoreBySlug() (src/firebase/products.js:270) and
//   getActiveStores() (:321) are collection queries that every public
//   storefront depends on.
// ============================================================================

rules_version = '2';
service cloud.firestore {
  match /databases/{database}/documents {

    // ------------------------------------------------------------------
    // Field groups locked against client writes on /stores/{storeId}.
    //
    // Rationale: `allow update: if request.auth.uid == storeId` permits the
    // owner to write ANY field, and server handlers read several of these as
    // authoritative facts. Without these locks a vendor could, from the
    // browser console: set their own `referralAvailable` and withdraw real
    // money (referral-withdraw-request.js:50), self-grant a premium plan
    // (store-write.js:58, staff-invites.js:79), or award themselves the
    // "CAC Verified" badge shown on their public storefront
    // (StoreNavbar.jsx:125), defeating the Prembly KYC flow.
    // ------------------------------------------------------------------

    // Money. Written only by paystack-webhook.js, referral-*.js and
    // admin-referrals.js — all server-side.
    function moneyFields() {
      return [
        'referralAvailable', 'referralPending', 'referralWithdrawn',
        'referralTotalEarned', 'referralCreditedToReferrer', 'referralTotalPaid',
        'referralTotalSignups', 'referralTotalClicks', 'referralCode',
        'referralBankVerified', 'referralBankName', 'referralBankAccountName',
        'referralBankAccountMasked', 'referralBankAccount', 'referralBankCode',
        'subaccountCode'
      ];
    }

    // Identity / verification. Written only by verify-cac.js, admin-cac.js and
    // phone-verify.js — all server-side.
    //
    // The phone* fields matter as much as the cac* ones: without them locked, a
    // vendor could set `phoneVerified: true` from the browser console and award
    // themselves the verified badge, skipping the SMS code entirely AND the
    // one-number-one-account claim in verifiedPhones. Same hole, same fix.
    function verificationFields() {
      return [
        'cacVerified', 'cacVerifiedAt', 'cacBusinessName', 'cacRcNumber',
        'cacStatus', 'cacRegistrationDate', 'cacRetryCount', 'cacLastRetryAt',
        'cacRejectedAt', 'cacRejectionReason', 'cacAttempts', 'cacDocType',
        'rcNumber',
        'phoneVerified', 'phoneVerifiedAt', 'phoneVerifiedMasked', 'verifiedPhone',
        // Proof that the account's email was confirmed by an emailed OTP.
        // Written only by login-verify.js. The storefront email gate trusts
        // this, so a client that could set it would self-publish an unverified
        // store, which is the whole thing the gate exists to stop.
        'emailVerifiedAt',
        // Grandfathers stores that existed before the storefront gates.
        // MUST be server-only: if a client could set either, a bot would simply
        // exempt itself and the gates would be decorative.
        'phoneGateExempt', 'gateExempt',
        // Early access to the Dropshipping Marketplace while DROPSHIPPING_STAGE
        // is 'testing'. Server set only: if a vendor could write it, the lock
        // that keeps a half-built marketplace out of reach would be decorative.
        'marketplaceTester',
        // Dropshipping Marketplace supplier approval (Phase 1). These decide
        // whose stock other people's stores are allowed to sell, so they carry
        // the same rule as cacVerified: Sellapage states them after a human has
        // reviewed the video and the checks, and the vendor cannot. A writable
        // supplierStatus is a vendor approving themselves.
        'supplierStatus', 'supplierAppliedAt', 'supplierApprovedAt',
        'supplierRejectedAt', 'supplierRejectionReason', 'supplierSuspendedAt',
        'supplierSuspendedReason', 'supplierVideoUrl', 'supplierApplicationNotes',
        'supplierDecidedBy', 'supplierDecidedAt',
        // Which version of the supplier's terms is current (Phase 2). Every
        // dropshipper acceptance is recorded against a version; a vendor who
        // could rewind this could make dropshippers "have accepted" terms
        // they never saw.
        'supplierTermsVersion', 'supplierTermsUpdatedAt',
        // Server-only, written ONLY by store-seo.js on a slug change. It holds
        // addresses this store used to own, and the storefront renderer 301s
        // them to the current one. A client that could write it would add
        // someone else's slug and capture that address the moment its owner
        // renamed, so it is locked here and derived from Firestore there.
        'previousSlugs'
      ];
    }

    // Third-party integrations. Written only by google-ads-*.js,
    // *-custom-domain.js and tiktok-*.js.
    //
    // The tiktok* entries below are the CONNECTION state, not the pixel.
    // They render on the public storefront as social proof (follower count,
    // verified tick, the vendor's actual videos), so they carry the same rule
    // as cacVerified: the platform states them, the vendor cannot. A writable
    // tiktokFollowerCount is a vendor typing their own social proof; a writable
    // tiktokVideos is a vendor putting arbitrary image urls and outbound links
    // on a page their customers trust.
    //
    // `tiktokPixelId` is intentionally absent: it is public by design, is not a
    // claim about anything, and is written from the browser exactly like
    // metaPixelId. The Events API token is not here either, because it never
    // touches this document - it lives in private/tiktokAds.
    function integrationFields() {
      return [
        'googleAdsConnected', 'googleAdsCustomerId', 'googleAdsRefreshToken',
        'googleAdsAccountName', 'googleAdsCurrency', 'googleAdsTimezone',
        'customDomain', 'customDomainStatus', 'customDomainAddedAt',
        'tiktokConnected', 'tiktokConnectedAt', 'tiktokUsername',
        'tiktokAvatarUrl', 'tiktokProfileLink', 'tiktokIsVerified',
        'tiktokFollowerCount', 'tiktokLikesCount', 'tiktokVideoCount',
        'tiktokVideos', 'tiktokVideosSyncedAt', 'tiktokStatsSyncedAt'
      ];
    }

    // Plan entitlements. Written only by paystack-webhook.js (on payment),
    // expiry-cron.js (on downgrade) and admin-trials.js (on a granted trial).
    //
    // `trial` is in this list for the same reason as the rest: it carries the
    // snapshot of the plan the vendor goes back to when the trial ends. A
    // vendor who could edit it could hand themselves a paid plan they never
    // bought, simply by rewriting what they are "restored" to.
    function planFields() {
      return [
        'plan', 'planStatus', 'planStartDate', 'planEndDate', 'graceUntil',
        'maxProducts', 'maxImagesPerProduct', 'maxJobListings',
        'hasGrowthFeatures', 'hasProFeatures', 'hasPremiumFeatures',
        'subscriptionStatus', 'trial', 'billingPeriod'
      ];
    }

    // Sella AI consent state. sellaStaffAccess decides whether people who are
    // NOT the account holder can send this store's data - including customer
    // names, phone numbers and addresses - to third-party AI providers. Every
    // flip is audit-logged server-side in sella-ai.js (action: staff-access),
    // and that record is the evidence of consent under NDPA 2023. Leaving the
    // field client-writable would let the owner flip it straight through the
    // SDK and leave no trace, so it is server-only by rule, not by convention.
    function aiFields() {
      return ['sellaStaffAccess'];
    }

    // Store Design (Premium). Written ONLY by /api/store-design, which runs on
    // the Admin SDK and therefore bypasses these rules entirely.
    //
    // Locked against direct client writes because that handler is where the
    // plan gate and the sanitiser live. Without this, a vendor on any plan
    // could write storeDesign straight from the browser SDK: skipping the
    // Premium check, and storing unsanitised colours, urls and section data
    // that then render on a public storefront.
    function designFields() {
      return ['storeDesign'];
    }

// Dropshipping Marketplace fields on a product (Phase 2). Mirrors
    // MARKETPLACE_PRODUCT_FIELDS in src/utils/marketplace.js: keep them the
    // same. Written only by /api/marketplace-listing on the Admin SDK, where
    // the wholesale price, category and NAFDAC number are validated. The
    // dropshipper-copy fields (dropshipped, source*) are locked now, before
    // Phase 3 trusts them, so nobody can mark their own product as another
    // supplier's in the meantime.
    function marketplaceProductFields() {
      return [
        'marketplaceListed', 'marketplaceStatus', 'wholesalePrice', 'minSellingPrice',
        'marketplaceCategory', 'marketplaceSubcategory', 'nafdacNumber', 'listingVersion',
        'marketplaceListedAt', 'marketplaceUpdatedAt',
        'dropshipped', 'sourceSupplierId', 'sourceProductId', 'marketplaceImportId', 'termsVersionAccepted'
      ];
    }

    function lockedStoreFields() {
      return moneyFields()
        .concat(verificationFields())
        .concat(integrationFields())
        .concat(planFields())
        .concat(aiFields())
        .concat(designFields());
    }

    match /stores/{storeId} {
      // PUBLIC READ — required by public store pages, and `list` is required by
      // getStoreBySlug()/getActiveStores(). Safe ONLY because credentials and
      // full bank details live in stores/{id}/private/* (store-secrets.js).
      // NEVER write a secret onto this document.
      allow read: if true;

      // CREATE — CLOSED to every client (2026-09-18). A store is created ONLY
      // by /api/signup-phone on the Admin SDK, after the vendor's SMS code has
      // been verified with Termii. That handler writes the store already
      // phone-verified and claims the number in verifiedPhones in the same
      // transaction. Neither the website nor the Android app may create a
      // store document directly any more: a direct create would skip the
      // phone code, the one-number-one-store claim and the 3-a-day SMS caps,
      // which is exactly how a bot signup would get in.
      //
      // The Android app's old signup wrote this document from the client SDK;
      // it must call /api/signup-phone (send, then complete) and sign in with
      // the custom token it returns. See Docs/App-Phone-Signup-Handoff.md.
      allow create: if false;

      // UPDATE — the owner may edit their own store, but never the locked
      // fields. affectedKeys() inspects only what actually changed, so an
      // unchanged locked field passing through a form payload is still fine.
      allow update: if request.auth != null
                    && request.auth.uid == storeId
                    && !request.resource.data.diff(resource.data)
                          .affectedKeys().hasAny(lockedStoreFields());

      allow delete: if request.auth != null && request.auth.uid == storeId;

      // Staff access to everything below is intentionally NOT granted here.
      // Staff act through firebase-admin API handlers, which bypass rules
      // entirely and check permissions via resolveStoreAccess(). Owner-only
      // here is the correct, deliberate posture - do not loosen it.

      // Server-only: OAuth refresh tokens, full bank account numbers, and the
      // pending CAC verification stash consumed by verify-cac?action=confirm.
      match /private/{docId} {
        allow read, write: if false;
      }

      match /products/{productId} {
        allow read: if true;
        // The owner keeps full control of their products EXCEPT the
        // marketplace fields (marketplaceProductFields above). A create may
        // not carry any of them; an update may not change, add or remove any
        // of them. The product form uses partial updates, so a listed
        // product's untouched fields never count as changed.
        allow create: if request.auth != null && request.auth.uid == storeId
          && !request.resource.data.keys().hasAny(marketplaceProductFields());
        allow update: if request.auth != null && request.auth.uid == storeId
          && !request.resource.data.diff(resource.data).affectedKeys().hasAny(marketplaceProductFields());
        allow delete: if request.auth != null && request.auth.uid == storeId;

        match /reviews/{reviewId} {
          allow read: if true;
          allow write: if false;
        }

        match /variations/{variantId} {
          allow read: if true;
          allow write: if request.auth != null && request.auth.uid == storeId;
        }
      }

      match /categories/{categoryId} {
        allow read: if true;
        allow write: if request.auth != null && request.auth.uid == storeId;
      }

      match /services/{serviceId} {
        allow read: if true;
        allow write: if request.auth != null && request.auth.uid == storeId;

        match /reviews/{reviewId} {
          allow read: if true;
          allow write: if false;
        }
      }

      // Orders and bookings are financial records. Create and update only,
      // never delete. Removing one silently corrupts revenue reporting, the
      // ledger, and loyalty reversal (which reads loyaltyEarned back off the
      // order). The dashboard delete buttons were removed on 2026-09-05; this
      // closes the browser-console path that removing a button does not.
      //
      // Support-driven deletion is still possible: the Admin SDK bypasses rules
      // entirely, so a server handler can do it deliberately and leave a trail.
      match /orders/{orderId} {
        allow read: if request.auth != null && request.auth.uid == storeId;
        allow create, update: if request.auth != null && request.auth.uid == storeId;
        allow delete: if false;
      }

      match /bookings/{bookingId} {
        allow read: if request.auth != null && request.auth.uid == storeId;
        allow create, update: if request.auth != null && request.auth.uid == storeId;
        allow delete: if false;
      }

      // Anonymous visitors on public store pages must be able to increment
      // counters, so writes stay unauthenticated — but are restricted to the
      // known counter fields AND to monotonic single-step increments. Without
      // the value bounds, anyone could set totalViews to an arbitrary number or
      // hammer this endpoint to burn the Spark plan's daily write quota.
      // `productClicks` and `serviceClicks` split what used to be one
      // `totalClicks` number. `totalClicks` is still written so a vendor's
      // all-time figure stays continuous; the two new fields are the honest
      // breakdown. Adding them here is NOT optional: hasOnly() rejects the
      // whole write if a key is missing from this list, and because the client
      // swallows analytics errors the failure would be completely silent.
      function summaryCounters() {
        return ['totalViews', 'totalClicks', 'productClicks', 'serviceClicks',
                'engagedViews', 'totalBookingRequests', 'updatedAt'];
      }

      // DELIBERATELY NOT listed above, and deliberately not in the daily
      // allowlist either: totalOrders, totalBookings, and the daily `orders`
      // and `bookings`. Sales are no longer stored as counters at all: the
      // dashboard counts the order and booking documents directly
      // (src/utils/sales.js). These names stay out of the allowlist anyway, so
      // a visitor can never plant a fake sales figure that later code trusts.

      // The owner resetting their own analytics to zero. This was missing, so
      // "Reset All Analytics" wrote 0 over a non-zero counter, failed the
      // monotonic check below, and rolled back the whole batch. The button
      // spun and then quietly did nothing.
      function isStoreOwner() {
        return request.auth != null && request.auth.uid == storeId;
      }

      match /analytics/{docId} {
        allow read: if isStoreOwner();

        allow create: if isStoreOwner()
                      || (request.resource.data.keys().hasOnly(summaryCounters())
                      && request.resource.data.get('totalViews', 0) <= 1
                      && request.resource.data.get('totalClicks', 0) <= 1
                      && request.resource.data.get('productClicks', 0) <= 1
                      && request.resource.data.get('serviceClicks', 0) <= 1
                      && request.resource.data.get('engagedViews', 0) <= 1
                      && request.resource.data.get('totalBookingRequests', 0) <= 1);

        allow update: if isStoreOwner()
                      || (request.resource.data.diff(resource.data).affectedKeys()
                           .hasOnly(summaryCounters())
                      && request.resource.data.get('totalViews', 0)
                           >= resource.data.get('totalViews', 0)
                      && request.resource.data.get('totalViews', 0)
                           <= resource.data.get('totalViews', 0) + 1
                      && request.resource.data.get('totalClicks', 0)
                           >= resource.data.get('totalClicks', 0)
                      && request.resource.data.get('totalClicks', 0)
                           <= resource.data.get('totalClicks', 0) + 1
                      && request.resource.data.get('productClicks', 0)
                           >= resource.data.get('productClicks', 0)
                      && request.resource.data.get('productClicks', 0)
                           <= resource.data.get('productClicks', 0) + 1
                      && request.resource.data.get('serviceClicks', 0)
                           >= resource.data.get('serviceClicks', 0)
                      && request.resource.data.get('serviceClicks', 0)
                           <= resource.data.get('serviceClicks', 0) + 1
                      && request.resource.data.get('engagedViews', 0)
                           >= resource.data.get('engagedViews', 0)
                      && request.resource.data.get('engagedViews', 0)
                           <= resource.data.get('engagedViews', 0) + 1
                      && request.resource.data.get('totalBookingRequests', 0)
                           >= resource.data.get('totalBookingRequests', 0)
                      && request.resource.data.get('totalBookingRequests', 0)
                           <= resource.data.get('totalBookingRequests', 0) + 1);

        allow delete: if false;
      }

      // Per-day counters, one document per Lagos calendar day, id "YYYY-MM-DD".
      //
      // Same shape of protection as the all-time summary: anonymous visitors
      // may only nudge a known counter by one, so nobody can invent a day, set
      // a view count to a million, or write arbitrary fields into a document a
      // vendor reads in their dashboard. `date` is a plain string copy of the
      // id, which is what the ordered query reads.
      //
      // Unlike the summary, the owner MAY delete these: resetting analytics
      // should remove the history, not leave a trail of zeroed days.
      match /analyticsDaily/{day} {
        allow read: if isStoreOwner();

        allow create: if isStoreOwner()
                      || (request.resource.data.keys().hasOnly(
                           ['date', 'views', 'productClicks', 'serviceClicks',
                            'bookingRequests', 'engagedSessions', 'updatedAt'])
                      && request.resource.data.date is string
                      && request.resource.data.date == day
                      && day.matches('^[0-9]{4}-[0-9]{2}-[0-9]{2}$')
                      && request.resource.data.get('views', 0) <= 1
                      && request.resource.data.get('productClicks', 0) <= 1
                      && request.resource.data.get('serviceClicks', 0) <= 1
                      && request.resource.data.get('bookingRequests', 0) <= 1
                      && request.resource.data.get('engagedSessions', 0) <= 1);

        allow update: if isStoreOwner()
                      || (request.resource.data.diff(resource.data).affectedKeys().hasOnly(
                           ['date', 'views', 'productClicks', 'serviceClicks',
                            'bookingRequests', 'engagedSessions', 'updatedAt'])
                      && request.resource.data.get('views', 0)
                           >= resource.data.get('views', 0)
                      && request.resource.data.get('views', 0)
                           <= resource.data.get('views', 0) + 1
                      && request.resource.data.get('productClicks', 0)
                           >= resource.data.get('productClicks', 0)
                      && request.resource.data.get('productClicks', 0)
                           <= resource.data.get('productClicks', 0) + 1
                      && request.resource.data.get('serviceClicks', 0)
                           >= resource.data.get('serviceClicks', 0)
                      && request.resource.data.get('serviceClicks', 0)
                           <= resource.data.get('serviceClicks', 0) + 1
                      && request.resource.data.get('bookingRequests', 0)
                           >= resource.data.get('bookingRequests', 0)
                      && request.resource.data.get('bookingRequests', 0)
                           <= resource.data.get('bookingRequests', 0) + 1
                      && request.resource.data.get('engagedSessions', 0)
                           >= resource.data.get('engagedSessions', 0)
                      && request.resource.data.get('engagedSessions', 0)
                           <= resource.data.get('engagedSessions', 0) + 1);

        allow delete: if isStoreOwner();
      }

      match /subscriptions/{subId} {
        allow read: if request.auth != null && request.auth.uid == storeId;
        allow write: if false;
      }

      match /discounts/{discountId} {
        allow read, write: if request.auth != null && request.auth.uid == storeId;
      }

      match /customers/{customerId} {
        allow read, write: if request.auth != null && request.auth.uid == storeId;
      }

      match /ledger/{entryId} {
        allow read, write: if request.auth != null && request.auth.uid == storeId;
      }

      match /sessions/{sessionId} {
        allow read: if request.auth != null && request.auth.uid == storeId;
        allow write: if false;
      }

      // Notification feed behind the bell in the mobile app. Written only by
      // the Admin SDK (_lib/notifications.js), so client writes are denied
      // outright - a vendor who could write here could forge an order alert.
      //
      // Owner read is direct. STAFF cannot read through this rule, because it
      // tests uid == storeId and a staff uid never matches; they go through
      // /api/notifications, which resolves store access server-side. That is
      // the same split as every other staff-visible collection.
      match /notifications/{notificationId} {
        allow read: if request.auth != null && request.auth.uid == storeId;
        allow write: if false;
      }

      // Per-store read state for PLATFORM BROADCASTS. Broadcast documents are
      // shared by every vendor, so "I have seen this one" cannot be stored on
      // the broadcast itself. Server-only: read state is set through
      // /api/notifications?action=read so staff and owner behave identically.
      match /meta/{docId} {
        allow read, write: if false;
      }

      // Staff role definitions - server-only (Admin SDK). Explicitly denied so
      // nobody later mistakes "no rule here" for an oversight.
      match /staffRoles/{roleId} {
        allow read, write: if false;
      }

      // Abandoned checkouts - server only. Holds a customer's name, email, phone
      // and the basket they walked away from, so it is never client-readable.
      // The vendor sees it through an authenticated handler, not directly.
      match /abandonedCheckouts/{reference} {
        allow read, write: if false;
      }

      // One doc per customer (id = sha256 of their email) holding the timestamp
      // of their last recovery email. Enforces the 24 hour throttle server side
      // so a vendor cannot bombard someone by pressing Send repeatedly.
      match /reminderThrottle/{emailHash} {
        allow read, write: if false;
      }

      // Loyalty cards - server only (Admin SDK via the loyalty-*.js handlers).
      // This one matters more than the others: the document id IS the customer's
      // loyalty code, and that code is a bearer token for money. Without this
      // rule, anyone could list the collection and read every code in the store.
      match /loyalty/{code} {
        allow read, write: if false;
      }

      // Sella AI Business Partner - server-only (written/read via Admin SDK,
      // which bypasses these rules). Clients are blocked from tampering with
      // their own daily usage count or reading any chat history directly.
      match /sellaAiUsage/{dateId} {
        allow read, write: if false;
      }

      match /sellaAiChats/{sessionId} {
        allow read, write: if false;

        // Text of files the vendor sent in this chat, kept for follow-ups.
        match /files/{fileId} {
          allow read, write: if false;
        }
      }

      // Monthly credit ledger and purchased top-ups. A vendor able to write
      // here could reset their own usage to zero or grant themselves credit.
      match /sellaCredits/{docId} {
        allow read, write: if false;
      }

      // What Sella remembers about the store, and saved prompts. Server-only:
      // memory steers every future answer, and the API allows only the owner
      // to change it. A client rule would let staff write it directly.
      match /sellaMemory/{memoryId} {
        allow read, write: if false;
      }

      match /sellaPrompts/{promptId} {
        allow read, write: if false;
      }
    }

    // Staff accounts - server-only (Admin SDK via staff-*.js handlers).
    // Explicitly denied: no client should ever query these directly.
    match /staffMemberships/{membershipId} {
      allow read, write: if false;
    }

    match /staffInvites/{code} {
      allow read, write: if false;
    }

    // Self-scoped: adminRoles.js only ever reads the caller's own doc, so a
    // vendor cannot enumerate the admin team and their roles.
    match /admins/{adminId} {
      allow read: if request.auth != null && request.auth.uid == adminId;
      allow write: if false;
    }

    // TIGHTENED 2026-08-09: was `read: if request.auth != null`, which let ANY
    // signed-in vendor read EVERY store's leads (customer PII). The precondition
    // this was waiting on has landed — Dashboard.jsx:543 now queries with
    // where('storeId','==',store.id), which satisfies the per-document rule.
    // Create stays open for anonymous storefront lead capture, but is now
    // constrained in shape and size so it can't be used to dump junk into the
    // collection or burn the daily write quota.
    // Field set verified against src/firebase/leads.js:10 (saveLead) and
    // src/components/LeadForm.jsx:23, which submits { name, phone, interest }
    // plus leadType/storeId/storeName/createdAt. `leadData` is a spread, so if
    // the form ever gains a field, add it here or creates will start failing.
    match /leads/{leadId} {
      // Created ONLY by /api/lead-submit on the Admin SDK (2026-09-18). The
      // old open create let any browser write a lead for any store, with any
      // storeName, and no server code ever saw it arrive, so the vendor was
      // never notified. The field bounds that lived here are now enforced in
      // lead-submit.js, with the same limits.
      allow create: if false;

      allow read: if request.auth != null
                  && request.auth.uid == resource.data.storeId;
    }

    // Nothing client-side reads waitlist — the admin panel uses the Admin SDK.
    // No writer was found in this repo either, so create is left open in shape
    // (an external landing-page form may still post here) and guarded only on
    // volume, to stop it being used to burn the Spark plan's daily write quota.
    match /waitlist/{id} {
      allow create: if request.resource.data.keys().size() <= 12
                    && request.resource.data.get('email', '').size() <= 320
                    && request.resource.data.get('name', '').size() <= 200;
      allow read: if false;
    }

    match /supportEnquiries/{enquiryId} {
      allow create: if request.auth != null
                    && request.resource.data.storeId == request.auth.uid;
      allow read: if request.auth != null && request.auth.uid == resource.data.storeId;
    }

    match /supportMessages/{messageId} {
      allow create: if request.auth != null
                    && request.resource.data.storeId == request.auth.uid;
      allow read: if request.auth != null && request.auth.uid == resource.data.storeId;
    }

    // ------------------------------------------------------------------
    // Server-only collections (Admin SDK). Listed explicitly rather than
    // relying on default-deny, so their absence is never read as an oversight.
    // ------------------------------------------------------------------

    // OAuth CSRF nonces (google-ads-auth.js / google-ads-callback.js).
    match /oauthStates/{state} {
      allow read, write: if false;
    }

    // Rate-limit counters (_lib/rate-limit.js).
    match /rateLimits/{bucket} {
      allow read, write: if false;
    }

    // One-signup-per-user referral attribution (referral-signup.js).
    match /referralAttributions/{uid} {
      allow read, write: if false;
    }

    // Payout requests — hold full bank account numbers. Admin SDK only.
    match /withdrawal_requests/{requestId} {
      allow read, write: if false;
    }

    match /referralRewards/{rewardId} {
      allow read, write: if false;
    }

    // Abuse reports, feature flags, cached bank list — Admin SDK only.
    // CAC registration help requests. Hold a vendor's email and phone, and are
    // listed across all stores by the admin panel, so top level and server only.
    match /cacRequests/{requestId} {
      allow read, write: if false;
    }

    // Investor and partner enquiries from the public /partners page. Hold
    // names, emails and phone numbers. Written by /api/partner-enquiry and read
    // by /api/admin-partners, both on the Admin SDK. Default deny would already
    // cover this; it is listed so nobody mistakes its absence for an oversight.
    match /partnerEnquiries/{enquiryId} {
      allow read, write: if false;
    }

    // Newsletter sign ups from the public footer box. Written by
    // /api/newsletter-subscribe and read by /api/admin-newsletter, both on the
    // Admin SDK. Client access denied outright: the document id IS the email
    // address, so a readable collection would be a harvestable mailing list.
    match /newsletterSubscribers/{subscriberId} {
      allow read, write: if false;
    }

    match /storeReports/{reportId} {
      allow read, write: if false;
    }

    match /featureFlags/{flagId} {
      allow read, write: if false;
    }

    match /cache/{docId} {
      allow read, write: if false;
    }

    // Vendor reminders, set through Sella and managed from the Reminders tab.
    //
    // SERVER ONLY. Every read and write goes through /api/reminders, which
    // verifies an id token and authorises via resolveStoreAccess, so the tab is
    // staff-assignable with per-role read/write. Client access is denied
    // outright because nextDueAt is the field the cron scans: a vendor who could
    // write it directly could schedule a reminder onto another store, and one
    // who could read the collection could read every other store's reminders.
    match /reminders/{reminderId} {
      allow read, write: if false;
    }

    // Sella background jobs (bulk imports). Server-only: a job document holds
    // the rows it will write, and the Admin SDK trusts them, so a client able
    // to edit one could write anything into any store. Progress is read
    // through /api/sella-ai action "job", which checks the store.
    match /sellaJobs/{jobId} {
      allow read, write: if false;
    }

    // Push device registry. One document per FCM token, id = sha256(token).
    //
    // SERVER ONLY, and that is the whole point of the collection existing.
    // Tokens used to live in an `fcmToken` field on stores/{storeId}, which is
    // `allow read: if true` because public storefronts read that document - so
    // every vendor's push token was world readable. Registration and unlinking
    // both go through /api/device-register and /api/device-unlink, which derive
    // storeId from a verified id token and never from the request body.
    match /devices/{deviceId} {
      allow read, write: if false;
    }

    // Platform broadcast history, written by /api/admin-push.
    //
    // Client read is denied deliberately. Each document carries the plan and
    // vendorType filters it was sent with, so a starter vendor reading this
    // collection directly could see a message targeted at Growth and Pro, and
    // the filters would be decoration rather than a rule. The app receives an
    // already-filtered, already-merged feed from /api/notifications instead.
    match /broadcasts/{broadcastId} {
      allow read, write: if false;
    }

    // Courier webhook evidence (2026-09-18), written by sendbox-webhook.js and
    // topship-webhook.js. A tally of every status code received plus, for
    // Topship, the latest raw payload per status. Server only: those payloads
    // carry customer names and delivery addresses. Read it in the Firebase
    // console, never from a client.
    match /webhookAudit/{docId} {
      allow read, write: if false;
    }

    // Admin > Email Broadcast (2026-09-18). All server only, written through
    // /api/admin-email, /api/email-broadcast-cron and /api/email-unsubscribe.
    //   emailCampaigns       drafts, schedules and each campaign's recipient
    //                        log (the recipients subcollection holds emails)
    //   emailSuppressions    everyone who unsubscribed from broadcasts
    //   emailQuota           per-day count of broadcast sends
    match /emailCampaigns/{campaignId} {
      allow read, write: if false;
      match /recipients/{recipientId} {
        allow read, write: if false;
      }
    }
    match /emailSuppressions/{emailKey} {
      allow read, write: if false;
    }
    match /emailQuota/{day} {
      allow read, write: if false;
    }

    // Phone-first signup (2026-09-18), written by /api/signup-phone. One
    // document per phone number being signed up: the Termii pinId, a hash of
    // the browser's one-time token, the email the code was sent for and the
    // attempt counter. Server only: a readable pinId plus the number is half
    // of a signup, and the email is personal data.
    match /signupChallenges/{phone} {
      allow read, write: if false;
    }

    // One verified number, one store (2026-08-12, now also written at signup).
    // Document id is the number, value is the store that owns it. Server only:
    // readable, it is a lookup from a phone number to a store; writable, a
    // vendor could release another store's number or claim one they never
    // verified. The instant "already verified" check on the signup form and in
    // Settings goes through /api/signup-phone and /api/phone-verify instead.
    match /verifiedPhones/{phone} {
      allow read, write: if false;
    }

    // Dropshipping Marketplace waitlist (2026-09-19, Phase 0), written by
    // /api/marketplace-waitlist and read by /api/admin-marketplace. Holds
    // names, emails and phone numbers of people who are not Sellapage
    // vendors yet, and the document id is the email address, so a readable
    // collection would be a harvestable contact list. Server only.
// A supplier's terms and every past version (Phase 2). SERVER ONLY: the
    // versions are the record of what each dropshipper agreed to, so they
    // must never be editable, even by the supplier who wrote them. Read
    // through /api/supplier-application (and the Phase 3 import dialog).
    match /supplierTerms/{supplierId} {
      allow read, write: if false;

      match /versions/{version} {
        allow read, write: if false;
      }
    }

    match /marketplaceWaitlist/{entryId} {
      allow read, write: if false;
    }
  }
}
